\begin{abstract}
The Berggren tree is the free ternary tree on the primitive Pythagorean triples, rooted at
$(3,4,5)$ and generated by three unimodular linear maps. We study a walk on this tree in which
a target integer $N$ defines an arithmetic \emph{energy} $E_N(a,b,c) = c \bmod N$, and call a
node \emph{resonant} when the energy vanishes, i.e.\ $N \mid c$. We prove four groups of
results. \textbf{(1)} The three branch maps preserve primitivity; every branch increases the
hypotenuse by at least $8$ and by at most a factor of $7$; the walk is a bijection between
finite words in a three-letter alphabet and the primitive Pythagorean triples with odd first
leg, so the depth-$n$ layer consists of exactly $3^n$ distinct triples. \textbf{(2)} For odd
$N>1$ a resonant node exists if and only if $-1$ is a quadratic residue modulo $N$; any two
resonant nodes satisfy $(a_1a_2)^2 \equiv (b_1b_2)^2 \pmod N$, so a non-degenerate resonant
pair yields the proper nontrivial divisor $\gcd(a_1a_2 - b_1b_2, N)$; and \emph{every} odd $N$
with all prime factors $\equiv 1 \pmod 4$ that is not a prime power carries two distinct nodes
of hypotenuse exactly $N$ whose interference gcd is an exact nontrivial proper divisor of $N$.
\textbf{(3)} The number $r(N)$ of nodes of hypotenuse exactly $N$ equals $1$ for every prime
power $p^k$ with $p \equiv 1 \pmod 4$ and exactly $2$ for a semiprime $pq$ with distinct
$p,q \equiv 1 \pmod 4$; hence unique resonance characterises prime powers, and multiplicity is
a primality certificate. \textbf{(4)} Since branch growth is bounded by the factor $7$ while
layer width grows as $3^n$, and $9>7$, any resonance for $N$ at depth $n$ forces
$3^n \ge \sqrt{N/5}$. The barrier is coin-independent: for an arbitrary complex amplitude
assignment $\psi$ the coherent resonance amplitude vanishes identically below the critical
depth and always obeys $|\mathcal A(\psi)|^2 \le |R|\,\|\psi\|^2$, with equality if and only if
$\psi$ is a scalar multiple of the indicator of the resonance set. On its admissible domain the
mechanism is an exact factoring oracle with no heuristic component; the sole obstruction to
efficiency is the geometry of the state space.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Pythagorean triples, Berggren tree, congruence of squares, sums of
two squares, integer factorisation, quantum walk, interference bound, rigidity.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}

Every factoring algorithm of the modern era is a machine for producing congruences of squares:
numbers $x,y$ with
\[
x^2 \equiv y^2 \pmod N, \qquad x \not\equiv \pm y \pmod N,
\]
from which $\gcd(x-y,N)$ is a proper divisor. Pythagorean triples manufacture such congruences
for free: if $a^2+b^2=c^2$ and $N \mid c$, then $a^2 \equiv -b^2 \pmod N$, and two such triples
multiply into a congruence of squares. The question is where to find the triples, and the
answer is one of the prettiest structures in elementary number theory: the Berggren ternary
tree, in which every primitive Pythagorean triple occurs exactly once.

This suggests a physical picture. Regard the tree as the configuration space of a
three-outcome walk, and attach to each node the arithmetic energy $E_N(t) = c \bmod N$. The
minima of the spectrum are the resonant nodes, and a coherent walker whose amplitude
concentrated there would, upon measurement, deliver a pair of resonant nodes and hence a factor
of $N$. This paper determines, exactly and without heuristics, how much of that picture is
true.

\subsection{Organisation}

Section~\ref{sec:tree} sets up the tree and proves primitivity preservation, two-sided growth
and the completeness bijection. Section~\ref{sec:res} introduces resonance and proves the
collapse theorem and the exact dichotomy governing which targets resonate.
Section~\ref{sec:universal} proves the universal collapse theorem.
Section~\ref{sec:mult} computes resonance multiplicities and derives the primality
certificate. Section~\ref{sec:barrier} proves the search barrier, its coin-independent form,
and the rigidity of the interference bound. Section~\ref{sec:algo} gives algorithms and
complexity, Section~\ref{sec:disc} discusses the results, and Section~\ref{sec:future} lists
open problems.

\section{The Berggren tree as a state space}\label{sec:tree}

\subsection{Definitions}

\begin{definition}[Node]
A \emph{node} is an ordered triple $t = (a,b,c)$ of integers; we write $t.a, t.b, t.c$ for its
coordinates.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Primitive Pythagorean triple]
A node $t$ is a \emph{primitive Pythagorean triple} (PPT) if $a^2+b^2=c^2$, all coordinates are
positive, and $\gcd(a,b)=1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Branch operators]
The three Berggren maps are given by the unimodular matrices
\[
A = \begin{pmatrix}1&-2&2\\2&-1&2\\2&-2&3\end{pmatrix},\quad
B = \begin{pmatrix}1&2&2\\2&1&2\\2&2&3\end{pmatrix},\quad
C = \begin{pmatrix}-1&2&2\\-2&1&2\\-2&2&3\end{pmatrix},
\]
acting on column vectors $(a,b,c)^{\mathsf T}$. We index them by $i \in \{0,1,2\}$ and write
$\br_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Walk]
A \emph{word} is a finite list $w = (i_1,\dots,i_n)$ with $i_j \in \{0,1,2\}$. The walk map is
$\walk(\,) = (3,4,5)$ and $\walk(i :: w) = \br_i(\walk(w))$: the word is read right to left
from the root. The \emph{depth} of $w$ is its length $|w|$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Invariance and growth}

\begin{proposition}[Primitivity is preserved]\label{prop:prim}
If $t$ is a PPT then $\br_i(t)$ is a PPT for each $i$. Consequently $\walk(w)$ is a PPT for
every word $w$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The Pythagorean relation is preserved by a direct identity for each matrix; e.g.\ for $A$,
\[
(a-2b+2c)^2 + (2a-b+2c)^2 - (2a-2b+3c)^2 = -(a^2+b^2-c^2).
\]
Positivity follows from the elementary estimates below. Coprimality is preserved because the
matrices are unimodular: writing the source coordinates as integral combinations of the image
coordinates, a common prime factor $p$ of the image legs divides the image hypotenuse (as
$p^2 \mid a'^2+b'^2 = c'^2$) and hence both source legs, contradicting primitivity of $t$.
\end{proof}

For a PPT $t$ one has $a,b \ge 3$, $c \ge 5$, and the \emph{skewness estimates}
\[
c+a-b \ \ge\ 4, \qquad c+b-a \ \ge\ 4,
\]
proved by checking the boundary cases $a = 3$ and $b = 3$ and otherwise squaring.

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided growth law]\label{thm:growth}
For every PPT $t$ and every $i \in \{0,1,2\}$,
\[
t.c + 8 \ \le\ \br_i(t).c \ \le\ 7\,t.c .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The three hypotenuse rows are $2a-2b+3c$, $2a+2b+3c$, $-2a+2b+3c$. The lower bounds reduce via
the skewness estimates to $2(c+a-b)+c \ge c+8$ and its mirror, and to $2a+2b+3c \ge c+8$. The
upper bounds follow from $a<c$ and $b<c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Depth window]\label{cor:window}
For every word $w$, $\;8|w| + 5 \le \walk(w).c \le 5\cdot 7^{|w|}$. Equivalently, a node of
hypotenuse $c$ occurs only at depths $n$ with $\log_7(c/5) \le n \le (c-5)/8$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[The slow branch is quadratic]
$A^n(3,4,5) = (2n+3,\;2n^2+6n+4,\;2n^2+6n+5)$, so hypotenuse $c$ is reachable at depth
$\Theta(\sqrt c)$.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{Completeness}

\begin{theorem}[Berggren bijection]\label{thm:bij}
The map $w \mapsto \walk(w)$ is injective, and its image is exactly the set of PPTs with odd
first leg. Every primitive Pythagorean triple, written with the odd leg first, therefore has a
unique address in $\{0,1,2\}^{*}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each branch preserves oddness of the first leg and the root has $a=3$. For surjectivity, define
candidate parent maps $\mathrm{par}_A,\mathrm{par}_B,\mathrm{par}_C$ by the inverse matrices;
each satisfies $\br_X(\mathrm{par}_X(t)) = t$ identically. For a PPT $t \ne (3,4,5)$ with odd
first leg put $u = a+2b-2c$ and $v = 2a+b-2c$. Then $u \ne 0$ by parity; $v = 0$ forces
$t=(3,4,5)$; $u,v$ are not both negative; and the sign pattern of $(u,v)$ selects exactly one
parent map returning a PPT of strictly smaller hypotenuse, indeed
$\mathrm{par}_X(t).c = -2a-2b+3c < c$. Descent on $c$ terminates at the root. Injectivity
follows because each parent map inverts the corresponding branch on the nose.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Layer size]\label{cor:layer}
The depth-$n$ layer consists of exactly $3^n$ distinct nodes.
\end{corollary}

\section{Resonance and collapse}\label{sec:res}

\begin{definition}
For a target $N$ and node $t$ the \emph{energy} is $E_N(t) = t.c \bmod N$; the node is
\emph{resonant} when $N \mid t.c$. The depth-$n$ \emph{resonance set} is
\[
R_N^{(n)} = \bigl\{\, r \in \{0,1,2\}^n : N \mid \walk(r).c \,\bigr\},
\]
and the \emph{multiplicity} is $r(N) = \#\{ w : \walk(w).c = N\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Congruence of squares]\label{lem:cong}
Let $N>1$ and $x,y \in \mathbb Z$ with $N \mid x^2-y^2$, $N \nmid x-y$, $N \nmid x+y$. Then
$g = \gcd(x-y,N)$ satisfies $1 < g < N$ and $g \mid N$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$g \mid N$ and $g > 0$. If $g = 1$ then $x-y$ is invertible mod $N$ and $N \mid (x-y)(x+y)$
gives $N \mid x+y$. If $g = N$ then $N \mid x-y$. Both contradict the hypotheses, and $g > N$
is impossible for a positive divisor of $N > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Resonant pair identity]\label{lem:pair}
If $a_i^2+b_i^2=c_i^2$ for $i=1,2$ and $N \mid c_1$, $N \mid c_2$, then
\[
(a_1a_2)^2 - (b_1b_2)^2 = a_2^2 c_1^2 - b_1^2 c_2^2,
\]
so $N$ (indeed $N^2$) divides the left-hand side.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Substitute $a_1^2 = c_1^2-b_1^2$ and $b_2^2 = c_2^2-a_2^2$ and expand.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Resonance collapse]\label{thm:collapse}
Let $N>1$ and let $t_1,t_2$ be PPTs with $N \mid t_1.c$, $N \mid t_2.c$. If
$N \nmid a_1a_2 - b_1b_2$ and $N \nmid a_1a_2+b_1b_2$, then
$\gcd(a_1a_2-b_1b_2, N)$ is a proper nontrivial divisor of $N$; in particular $N$ is composite.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Lemmas~\ref{lem:cong} and~\ref{lem:pair} with $x = a_1a_2$, $y = b_1b_2$.
\end{proof}

We call such a pair \emph{non-degenerate} and call $\gcd(a_1a_2-b_1b_2,N)$ its
\emph{interference gcd}.

\begin{example}[$N=65$]
The words $(0,2)$ and $(2,2,2)$ give the nodes $(33,56,65)$ and $(63,16,65)$. Here
$a_1a_2 = 2079$, $b_1b_2 = 896$, and $2079^2-896^2 = 1183\cdot 2975 = 3\,519\,425 =
65 \cdot 54\,145$, so $2079^2 \equiv 896^2 \pmod{65}$, and
\[
\gcd(2079-896,\;65) = \gcd(1183,65) = 13 ,
\]
whence $65 = 13 \cdot 5$.
\end{example}

\begin{theorem}[Resonance dichotomy]\label{thm:dich}
Let $N>1$ be odd. A word $w$ with $N \mid \walk(w).c$ exists if and only if $-1$ is a square in
$\mathbb Z/N\mathbb Z$. When one exists, it can be chosen with hypotenuse at most $(N-1)^2+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) If $t$ is resonant then $a^2+b^2 \equiv 0 \pmod N$ with $\gcd(a,b)=1$, so $b$
is invertible mod $N$ and $(ab^{-1})^2 \equiv -1$.
($\Leftarrow$) Let $s^2 \equiv -1 \pmod N$, chosen even (possible since $N$ is odd). Then
$(s^2-1,\,2s,\,s^2+1)$ is a PPT with odd first leg and $N \mid s^2+1$, and by
Theorem~\ref{thm:bij} it is a node of the tree. Taking $0 \le s \le N-1$ bounds the hypotenuse.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Invisible targets]
If a prime $p \equiv 3 \pmod 4$ divides $N$, then no node of the tree is resonant for $N$.
\end{corollary}

The \emph{admissible domain} is therefore
$\mathcal D = \{N>1 : N \text{ odd, all prime factors} \equiv 1 \bmod 4\}$.

\section{Universal collapse}\label{sec:universal}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{primitive representation} of $M$ is a pair $(A,B)$ with $A,B>0$, $\gcd(A,B)=1$ and
$A^2+B^2 = M$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Brahmagupta composition]\label{lem:brah}
If $x^2+y^2 = m$ and $u^2+v^2 = n$ then
\[
(xu-yv)^2+(xv+yu)^2 = mn, \qquad (xu+yv)^2+(xv-yu)^2 = mn .
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Composition preserves primitivity across coprime parts]\label{lem:brahprim}
If $(x,y)$ is a primitive representation of an odd $m$, $(u,v)$ one of an odd $n$, and
$\gcd(m,n)=1$, then both compositions of Lemma~\ref{lem:brah} are primitive.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A prime $r$ dividing both coordinates of a composition satisfies $r^2 \mid mn$ and divides the
quartic combinations of the composed pair; using $\gcd(m,n)=1$ to localise $r$ in one factor,
$r$ divides both $x,y$ or both $u,v$, contradicting primitivity. Oddness excludes $r=2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Prime powers are primitively represented]\label{thm:pp}
For a prime $p \equiv 1 \pmod 4$ and $k \ge 1$, $p^k$ admits a primitive representation.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on $k$, the base case being Fermat's two-square theorem $p = x^2+y^2$ with
$\gcd(x,y)=1$. Given a primitive representation $(A,B)$ of $p^k$, the two compositions
$(xA-yB,\,xB+yA)$ and $(xA+yB,\,xB-yA)$ represent $p^{k+1}$. They cannot both be imprimitive:
a prime $r$ dividing all four coordinates divides $2xA$, $2yB$, $2xB$, $2yA$, and with
$r \ne 2$, $\gcd(A,B) = \gcd(x,y) = 1$ this forces $r \mid p$ and then $p \mid A$, $p \mid B$,
which is impossible. Exactly one composition remains primitive (the other is divisible by $p$);
adjust signs to make both coordinates positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Every admissible modulus is primitively represented]\label{thm:allrep}
If $N>1$ is odd with all prime factors $\equiv 1 \pmod 4$, then $N$ admits a primitive
representation.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $N = \prod_j p_j^{k_j}$; each $p_j^{k_j}$ is primitively represented by
Theorem~\ref{thm:pp}, and Lemma~\ref{lem:brahprim} composes pairwise coprime odd factors by
induction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Coprime-split collapse]\label{thm:split}
Let $N = mn$ with $m,n>1$ odd and coprime, and let $(x,y)$, $(u,v)$ be primitive
representations of $m$ and $n$. Let $t_1,t_2$ be the nodes obtained from the two Brahmagupta
compositions via $\rho(A,B) = (A^2-B^2,\,2AB,\,A^2+B^2)$, with signs and order normalised so
that the first leg is odd and positive. Then $t_1 \ne t_2$, both have hypotenuse exactly $N$,
and the interference gcd equals $m$ (respectively $n$) exactly; in particular it is a
nontrivial proper divisor of $N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both compositions are primitive representations of $N$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:brahprim}, hence give
PPTs of hypotenuse $N$; they are distinct because the coordinate pairs differ (by $2yv$ and
$2yu$) and $m,n>1$ prevents degeneracy. The sign-interference identity expresses
$a_1a_2-b_1b_2$ as a product in which the $m$-part and the $n$-part separate, and two gcd
evaluation lemmas, using $\gcd(m,n)=1$ and the coprimality of the quartic combinations with $m$
and with $n$, identify $\gcd(a_1a_2-b_1b_2,N)$ exactly.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Universal resonance collapse]\label{thm:universal}
Let $N$ be odd with every prime factor $\equiv 1 \pmod 4$, and suppose $N$ has two distinct
prime divisors $p \ne q$. Then there exist words $w_1 \ne w_2$ with
$\walk(w_1).c = \walk(w_2).c = N$ such that, writing $t_i = \walk(w_i)$,
\[
1 \;<\; \gcd(a_1a_2-b_1b_2,\ N) \;<\; N, \qquad \gcd(a_1a_2-b_1b_2,\ N) \ \big|\ N .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $k = v_p(N)$ and split $N = m\cdot n$ with $m = p^k$, $n = N/p^k$; then $m,n>1$ (as
$q \mid n$), coprime and odd, with all prime factors $\equiv 1 \bmod 4$. Apply
Theorems~\ref{thm:pp}, \ref{thm:allrep} and~\ref{thm:split}, then Theorem~\ref{thm:bij} to
convert nodes into words.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Explicit factorisation]
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:universal} there are $d,e>1$ with $de=N$ and
$d = \gcd(a_1a_2-b_1b_2,N)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Depth window of the collapsing pair]
The two words satisfy $\log_7(N/5) \le |w_i| \le (N-5)/8$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:window}.
\end{corollary}

\noindent\textbf{Worked instances.} $N = 325 = 5^2\cdot 13$ (non-squarefree) collapses via
$(253,204,325)$ and $(323,36,325)$ with interference gcd $25$; $N = 1105 = 5\cdot13\cdot17$
collapses with gcd $221$; $N = 32045 = 5\cdot13\cdot17\cdot29$ has eight nodes of hypotenuse
$N$ and collapses with gcd $1105$.

\section{Resonance multiplicity}\label{sec:mult}

By Theorem~\ref{thm:bij}, $r(N)$ equals the number of primitive representations
$N = A^2+B^2$ with $A$ odd and $A,B>0$.

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness for primes]\label{thm:uprime}
For a prime $p \equiv 1 \pmod 4$ there is exactly one word with hypotenuse $p$; $r(p)=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Existence is Fermat plus Theorem~\ref{thm:bij}. For uniqueness, if $A^2+B^2 = C^2+D^2 = p$ then
$p \mid (AD-BC)(AD+BC)$ while $p$ divides none of $A,B,C,D$, and
$(AC\mp BD)^2+(AD\pm BC)^2 = p^2$ forces one of $AD \mp BC$ to vanish, giving
$\{A^2,B^2\} = \{C^2,D^2\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness for prime powers]\label{thm:upp}
For a prime $p \equiv 1 \pmod 4$ and any $k \ge 1$ there is exactly one word with hypotenuse
$p^k$; $r(p^k)=1$, and no interference pair exists for a prime-power target.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Existence is Theorem~\ref{thm:pp}. Let $A^2+B^2 = A'^2+B'^2 = p^k$ with both pairs primitive;
then $p$ divides none of $A,B,A',B'$. From
\[
(AB'-A'B)(AB'+A'B) = p^k\,(B'^2-B^2)
\]
and the fact that $p$ divides neither the sum $2AB'$ nor the difference $2A'B$ of the two left
factors, all of $p^k$ divides one of them; the identity
$(AA'\mp BB')^2+(AB'\pm A'B)^2 = p^{2k}$ then forces that factor to be $0$ or $\pm p^k$. In the
first case $AB'=A'B$ and coprimality gives $A=A'$, $B=B'$; in the second the companion term
vanishes and coprimality gives $\{A,B\} = \{B',A'\}$, which the odd-first-leg normalisation
identifies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exactly two for a semiprime]\label{thm:semi}
For distinct primes $p,q \equiv 1 \pmod 4$ there are exactly two words with hypotenuse $pq$;
$r(pq)=2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Existence of two: the two Brahmagupta compositions, primitive by Lemma~\ref{lem:brahprim} and
distinct. At most two: a representation-descent lemma shows that if $x^2+y^2 = A^2+B^2 = pq$
then $p$ divides one of $xB \pm yA$; dividing through returns representations of $q$, where
Theorem~\ref{thm:uprime} applies, and sorting by sign pattern shows any third representation
coincides with one of the first two.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Multiplicity is a primality certificate]
For distinct primes $p,q \equiv 1 \bmod 4$, the target $p$ admits exactly one resonant word
while $pq$ admits more than one.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Prime-power dichotomy]\label{thm:dicho}
Let $N>1$ be odd with all prime factors $\equiv 1 \pmod 4$. Then exactly one of the following
holds: either $N = p^k$ is a prime power, the resonant word is unique and no interference pair
exists; or $N$ has two distinct prime divisors and some resonant pair collapses onto an exact
nontrivial proper divisor of $N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Every $N>1$ either has two distinct prime factors or is a prime power; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:universal} in the first case and Theorem~\ref{thm:upp} in the second.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unique resonance characterises prime powers]
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:dicho},
$\bigl(\exists!\,w:\ \walk(w).c = N\bigr) \iff N$ is a prime power.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}[General multiplicity formula]
Numerically $r(N) = 2^{\omega(N)-1}$, where $\omega$ counts distinct prime factors:
$r(5)=r(25)=r(125)=1$, $r(65)=r(325)=r(4225)=2$, $r(1105)=4$, $r(32045)=8$. The cases
$\omega = 1$ (all $k$) and $\omega = 2$ (squarefree) are established above; the general
statement is consistent with all computed data.
\end{remark}

\section{The barrier}\label{sec:barrier}

\begin{theorem}[Depth lower bound]\label{thm:depth}
If $N \mid \walk(w).c$ then $N \le 5\cdot 7^{|w|}$, i.e.\ $|w| \ge \log_7(N/5)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Search-space lower bound]\label{thm:search}
If $N \mid \walk(w).c$ then $N \le 5\,(3^{|w|})^2$. Hence if $R_N^{(n)} \ne \emptyset$ then
\[
3^n \ \ge\ \sqrt{N/5}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$7^n \le 9^n = (3^n)^2$ since $7<9$; combine with Theorem~\ref{thm:depth}.
\end{proof}

By Corollary~\ref{cor:layer} the depth-$n$ state space has dimension exactly $3^n$; at the
first depth where a resonance for $N$ can occur that dimension is already $\Omega(\sqrt N)$,
exponential in $\log N$.

\begin{corollary}[Grover-type reading]
$\sqrt{3^n} \ge (N/5)^{1/4}$: amplitude amplification over a single layer costs
$\Omega(N^{1/4})$.
\end{corollary}

Let $\psi : \{0,1,2\}^n \to \mathbb C$ be an arbitrary amplitude assignment --- no unitarity,
product structure or locality assumed, so uniform, biased, position-dependent, adaptive and
entangling coins are all covered. Put
\[
\mathcal A_N(\psi) = \sum_{r \in R_N^{(n)}} \psi_r, \qquad
\|\psi\|^2 = \sum_r |\psi_r|^2 .
\]

\begin{theorem}[Vanishing below the critical depth]
If $5\cdot 7^n < N$ then $R_N^{(n)} = \emptyset$ and $\mathcal A_N(\psi) = 0$ for every state
$\psi$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Interference bound]\label{thm:bound}
For every $n$, $N$ and $\psi$,
$\;|\mathcal A_N(\psi)|^2 \le |R_N^{(n)}|\cdot\|\psi\|^2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$|\mathcal A_N(\psi)| \le \sum_{r\in R}|\psi_r|$ by the triangle inequality;
$\bigl(\sum_{r\in R}|\psi_r|\bigr)^2 \le |R|\sum_{r\in R}|\psi_r|^2$ by Cauchy--Schwarz; and
$\sum_{r\in R}|\psi_r|^2 \le \|\psi\|^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The uniform coin assigns $3^{-n/2}$ to each branch, so its coherent resonance intensity is
$|R|^2 3^{-n}$, which equals $|R|$ times the classical resonance probability $|R|3^{-n}$ and
satisfies Theorem~\ref{thm:bound} as the instance $|R|^2 3^{-n} \le |R|$.
\end{remark}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{resonance-indicator state} with amplitude $c$ is $\iota_c(r) = c$ for
$r \in R_N^{(n)}$ and $0$ otherwise.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
$\mathcal A_N(\iota_c) = |R|c$ and $\|\iota_c\|^2 = |R||c|^2$, so
$|\mathcal A_N(\iota_c)|^2 = |R|\cdot\|\iota_c\|^2$: the bound is attained.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Rigidity]\label{thm:rigid}
Assume $R = R_N^{(n)} \ne \emptyset$. Then
\[
|\mathcal A_N(\psi)|^2 = |R|\cdot\|\psi\|^2
\iff
\exists c \in \mathbb C:\ \psi = \iota_c .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Only $\Rightarrow$ needs argument. Put $c = \mathcal A_N(\psi)/|R|$ and use the variance
identity, valid for any finite $S$, $f : S \to \mathbb C$ and $c$:
\[
\sum_{r\in S}|f(r)-c|^2
= \sum_{r\in S}|f(r)|^2 - 2\Real\Bigl(\bar c \sum_{r \in S} f(r)\Bigr) + |S|\,|c|^2 .
\]
With $S = R$, $f = \psi|_R$ and this $c$ the last two terms combine to
$-|\mathcal A_N(\psi)|^2/|R|$, giving
\[
\sum_{r\in R}|\psi_r - c|^2 = \sum_{r\in R}|\psi_r|^2 - \frac{|\mathcal A_N(\psi)|^2}{|R|} .
\]
Equality in Theorem~\ref{thm:bound} forces the right-hand side to be $\le 0$, hence $0$: $\psi$
is constant on $R$; the same inequality is tight only if $\psi$ vanishes off $R$.
\end{proof}

\noindent\textbf{Interpretation.} The unique optimal state, up to a global amplitude, is the
normalised indicator of the resonance set; preparing it presupposes knowledge of $R$, i.e.\ of
the answer. The obstruction is thus \emph{kinematic} --- a property of the support geometry of
the walk --- rather than dynamical.

\section{Algorithms and complexity}\label{sec:algo}

\paragraph{Address decoding.} Iterating the parent map of Theorem~\ref{thm:bij} reduces the
hypotenuse from $c$ to $-2a-2b+3c$, by at least $8$ each step, so the loop terminates in at
most $(c-5)/8$ iterations; typically the decrease is geometric and the count is $O(\log c)$,
with the worst case $O(\sqrt c)$ along the slow branch.

\paragraph{Resonance enumeration.} One may traverse the tree pruning at $c > N$ (correct
because the hypotenuse strictly increases, but with cost $\Theta(N)$), or enumerate primitive
representations $N = A^2+B^2$ directly ($O(\sqrt N)$ by trial, or polynomial time given the
factorisation of $N$). The second option exhibits the circularity that
Theorem~\ref{thm:dicho} already suggests.

\paragraph{Collapse.} Given two distinct resonant nodes, the collapse is one gcd:
$O(\log^2 N)$ bit operations.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
Task & Cost \\
\midrule
Apply one branch & $O(\log N)$ bit operations \\
Decode an address & $O(\log c)$ typical, $O(\sqrt c)$ worst-case steps \\
Verify resonance of a node & $O(\log^2 N)$ \\
Collapse a given pair & $O(\log^2 N)$ \\
Find a resonant pair by tree search & $\Omega(\sqrt N)$ layer dimension \\
Find a resonant pair via representations & $O(\sqrt N)$, or poly.\ given the factorisation \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Everything local is polynomial; the search is not, and Theorem~\ref{thm:search} says so
unconditionally.

\section{Discussion}\label{sec:disc}

\paragraph{The arithmetic is exact and universal on its domain.} For every odd $N$ with all
prime factors $\equiv 1 \bmod 4$ that is not a prime power, the tree contains a specific pair
of nodes of hypotenuse exactly $N$ whose interference gcd is an exact nontrivial proper
divisor. There is no ``with high probability'', no sieving and no smoothness assumption.
Conversely prime powers provably refuse to split, since they carry exactly one resonant node
and interference needs two.

\paragraph{The admissible domain is exactly characterised.} Resonance exists iff $-1$ is a
quadratic residue modulo $N$; a single prime factor $\equiv 3 \bmod 4$ makes the target
invisible to the entire infinite tree.

\paragraph{The efficiency claim is refuted structurally.} Values grow by at most $7$ per
branch while layers widen by $3$; because $9>7$, the first resonant layer has at least
$\sqrt{N/5}$ branches. Below the critical depth every state has zero resonance amplitude, and
at any depth the interference gain of every state is at most the arithmetic multiplicity
$|R|$. Rigidity completes the diagnosis: the unique optimal state is the normalised indicator
of the resonance set.

\paragraph{Relation to classical factorisation.} Theorem~\ref{thm:split} is Euler's
two-representation factorisation with two upgrades: an exact evaluation of the resulting gcd,
and an unconditional existence statement for the required pair, including in non-squarefree
cases. The tree adds a canonical finite address to each representation, turning the collapse
into interference between two addresses --- a genuine structural and expository gain that does
not change the computational difficulty, which is that of producing two essentially different
representations of $N$ as a sum of two squares.

\paragraph{Positive readings.} Two consequences survive independently of factoring. First, a
structural primality certificate: among admissible moduli, uniqueness of the resonant node is
equivalent to being a prime power. Second, a template for evaluating similar proposals:
compare the growth rate of values along branches with the branching factor, and check whether
the optimum-attaining state is knowledge-free.

\section{Future directions}\label{sec:future}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{General multiplicity formula.} Prove $r(N) = 2^{\omega(N)-1}$ for all admissible
$N$, extending the cases $\omega = 1$ and $\omega = 2$ proved here.
\item \textbf{Exact interference gcd for arbitrary splittings.} Characterise the set of
divisors obtainable as interference gcds over all resonant pairs, and determine when a prime
factor rather than a composite one is returned.
\item \textbf{Depth statistics.} Determine the distribution of address lengths of the resonant
nodes of hypotenuse exactly $N$. Empirically the two nodes of a semiprime sit at very
different depths (for $N = 325$: depths $5$ and $8$; for $N=32045$: depths $7$ and $45$).
\item \textbf{Beyond a single layer.} Analyse superpositions across many depths with
depth-dependent weights; the same growth law should yield an analogous, indeed stronger,
bound, since the number of nodes below the critical hypotenuse is $\Theta(N)$.
\item \textbf{Other Diophantine trees.} The argument used only unique addressing, growth rates
$g_{\min}, g_{\max}$ and a branching factor $b$: the barrier reads
$b^n \ge (N/c_0)^{\log b/\log g_{\max}}$. Identify trees with $g_{\max}$ large relative to $b$
and determine whether rigidity persists.
\item \textbf{Rigidity for approximate optima.} A stability version of
Theorem~\ref{thm:rigid} --- states within $\varepsilon$ of the bound lie within
$\delta(\varepsilon)$ of the indicator --- would close the last conceivable loophole.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

The Berggren tree is a complete, rigid state space for the primitive Pythagorean triples, and
the arithmetic energy $E_N(t) = c \bmod N$ turns it into a resonator for the target $N$. On the
domain where the resonator can sound at all, the mechanism is exact: every non-prime-power
target carries an interference pair whose gcd is an exact nontrivial proper divisor, and every
prime power carries exactly one resonant node and provably refuses to split, so multiplicity is
a primality certificate. The efficiency claim fails for a reason expressible in one line:
values grow by at most $7$ per branch while layers widen by $3$, and $9>7$, so the first
resonant layer has $\sqrt{N/5}$ branches. No coin can evade this, and the unique state
attaining the maximal interference gain is the indicator of the resonance set, whose
preparation already encodes the answer. The arithmetic always cooperates; the search space
never does.

